                                         Load Balancer

--> It is to distribute incoming network traffic across multiple backend resources,
 such as Azure Virtual Machines or Virtual Machine Scale Sets.

Important components
1. Frontend IP
--> This is the IP address through which users access the Load Balancer.
2. Backend Pool
--> The backend pool contains the resources that receive traffic.
3. Health Probe
--> The Load Balancer checks whether the backend servers are healthy.
4. Load-balancing Rule
--> The rule tells Azure how to forward traffic.

**Azure LB VS AWS ELB** :
1. Azure Load Balancer works at Layer 4, whereas AWS ELB can provide different types of load balancing, including Layer 4 through NLB and Layer 7 through ALB.
2. Azure Load Balancer supports TCP and UDP traffic, whereas AWS NLB supports TCP/UDP/TLS and AWS ALB handles HTTP/HTTPS at Layer 7.
3. Azure Load Balancer uses frontend IP configurations, backend pools, health probes, and load-balancing rules, whereas AWS ELB uses concepts such as listeners, target groups, and health checks.